@startuml qwen3tts-speedup
title <b>qwen3tts: what buys 5x realtime</b>\nthree corpora, 1612-4763 words, 100-201 segments, one M4 / 16 GB, f16, default settings

skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam titleFontSize 15
skinparam roundCorner 8
skinparam nodesep 45
skinparam ranksep 30
skinparam ArrowColor #666666
skinparam ArrowFontSize 11
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #6B7A88
  BackgroundColor #EDF1F5
}
skinparam noteFontSize 12

rectangle "text -> segments" as SEG
rectangle "ICL prompt\nref codes + spk" as PROMPT
rectangle "**scheduler**\n48 lanes, longest-first" as SCHED #DDF3E0
rectangle "**talker**\n0.104-0.116  (60%)" as TALKER #DDF3E0
rectangle "**codec**\n0.069-0.071  (40%)" as CODEC #DDF3E0
rectangle "24 kHz WAV\n**0.175-0.186 = 5.4-5.7x**" as WAV #D6E9F8

SEG --> PROMPT
PROMPT --> SCHED
SCHED --> TALKER : "48 lanes"
TALKER --> CODEC : "[T, 16] codes"
CODEC --> WAV

note right of SCHED #DDF3E0
  shape buckets
  length sort, <b>longest-first</b>
  chunk to 48
end note

note right of TALKER #DDF3E0
  f16, not q8_0      <b>7.4x</b> vs 1.1x
  batched decode     48 lanes, longest-first
  shed finished tail  68-70% -> 79-90% useful
  shed in 8s         one width set, not 48
  prefill in 8s      748 MB -> 94 MB of scores
  in-place KV        -112 MB/step
  folded tables      -126 MB/frame
end note

note right of CODEC #DDF3E0
  one call per utterance
  300 + 25 frame chunks
  <b>uniform span, padded right</b>
  fused NLC kernels
end note

note left of SCHED #FBDDDD
  <b>cliff at 56 lanes</b>
  not the lane count: 100 segments
  split 56 + 44, so the KV cache is
  allocated twice (4.2 + 3.3 GB)
  where 48 + 4 costs 3.6 + 0.3
end note

note left of TALKER #FDEBD0
  <i>beyond 5x</i>
  refill <b>interior</b> lanes
  (a tail is shed; a lane that
  finishes mid-batch is not)
end note

note left of CODEC #FDEBD0
  <i>beyond 5x</i>
  torch-parity kernels
  (1.7-2.2x behind torch)
end note

note bottom of WAV #FFFDF0
  |= corpus |= segs |= was (24) |= 48 lanes |= <b>shipped</b> |= |
  | book chapter, 3227w | 150 | | 0.195 | <b>0.175</b> | <b>5.7x</b> |
  | article, 4763w | 201 | 0.235 | 0.200 | <b>0.183</b> | <b>5.5x</b> |
  | chapter.txt, 1612w | 100 | 0.260 | 0.218 | <b>0.186</b> | <b>5.4x</b> |

  peak footprint 13.1-13.7 GB, from 14.2-14.8. Quality checked on the audio, not the codes:
  WER 0.046 -> <b>0.040</b> against the same text, median F0 and LTAS cosine identical to
  four decimals (173.9 Hz, 0.9973) against the clip the voice was cloned from.

  Shedding pays most where segment lengths vary most: the article's spread is the narrowest of
  the three (cv 0.37 against 0.55-0.57) and it gains the least. So these are a floor, not a
  best case.

  Memory is candle's Metal pool, which keys buffers by size and releases none: 1515 GPU
  allocations totalling 12.6 GB mid-render against ~4.5 GB of weights. Every memory win here is
  a *shape* removed, not bytes shaved.

  Per lane, in isolation, cost was still falling at 64 (~0.4 ms per added lane from 16 up). The
  wall is memory, not arithmetic: 48 lanes peak at 14.8 GB, 56 at 19.0 GB on a 16 GB machine.
  Canary 59.4 ms; the 24-lane baseline re-measured 0.235 -> 0.237 after 30 min of load, and 48
  re-measured 0.200 -> 0.202, so these are ratios within one thermal state.
  green measured, amber projected
end note

@enduml
